WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.530
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:10.160
This work is about performing useful image
restoration tasks with a convolutional neural

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.459
network with an additional twist.

00:00:12.459 --> 00:00:18.310
Its main use cases are as follows:
One, in the case of JPEG artifact removal,

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:24.080
the input is this image with many blocky artifacts
that materialized during compression, and

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.430
the output is a restored version of this image.

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:32.180
Two, image inpainting where some regions of
the input image are missing and are to be

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:35.930
filled with useful and hopefully plausible
information.

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:41.719
Three, super resolution where the input image
is intact, but is very coarse and has low

00:00:41.719 --> 00:00:46.600
resolution, and the output should be a more
detailed, higher resolution version of the

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:47.600
same image.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.110
This is the classic enhance scenario from
these CSI TV series.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:56.910
It is typically hard to do because there is
a stupendously large number of possible high

00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:01.020
resolution image solutions that we could come
up with as an output.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:04.559
Four, image denoising is also a possibility.

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:09.490
The standard way of doing these is that we
train such a network on a large database of

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:15.570
images so that they can learn the concept
of many object classes, such as humans, animals

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:21.700
and more, and also the typical features and
motifs that are used to construct such images.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:26.490
These networks have some sort of understanding
of these images and hence, can perform these

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:30.130
operations better than most handcrafted algorithms.

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:32.210
So let's have a look at some comparisons.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:38.130
Do you see these bold lettered labels that
classify these algorithms as trained or untrained?

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:44.009
The bicubic interpolation is a classical untrained
algorithm that almost naively tries to guess

00:01:44.009 --> 00:01:47.329
the pixel colors by averaging its neighbors.

00:01:47.329 --> 00:01:52.200
This is clearly untrained because it does
not take a database of images to learn on.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:57.429
Understandably, the fact that the results
are lackluster is to show that non-learning-based

00:01:57.429 --> 00:01:59.820
algorithms are not great at this.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:06.009
The SRResNet is a state of the art learning-based
technique for super resolution that was trained

00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:07.909
on a large database of input images.

00:02:07.909 --> 00:02:12.250
It is clearly doing way better than bicubic
interpolation.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:18.530
And look, here we have this Deep Prior algorithm
that performs comparably well, but is labeled

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:19.980
to be untrained.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.700
So what is going on here?

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:26.350
And here comes the twist - this Convolutional
Neural Network is actually untrained.

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:32.170
This means that the neuron weights are randomly
initialized, which generally leads to completely

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:34.550
useless results on most problems.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:39.349
So no aspect of this network works through
the data it has learned on, all the required

00:02:39.349 --> 00:02:43.590
information is contained within the structure
of the network itself.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:47.950
We all know that the structure of these neural
networks matter a great deal, but in this

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:52.659
case, it is shown that it is at least as important
as the training data itself.

00:02:52.659 --> 00:02:57.700
A very interesting and esoteric idea indeed,
please make sure to have a look at the paper

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:02.390
for details as there are many details to be
understood to get a more complete view of

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:03.390
this conclusion.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:09.650
In the comparisons, beyond the images, researchers
often publish this PSNR number you see for

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:10.650
each image.

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:16.170
This is the Peak Signal To Noise ratio, which
means how close the output image is to the

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:21.250
ground truth, and this number is, of course,
always subject to maximization.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:27.110
Remarkably, this untrained network performs
well both on images with natural patterns

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:29.049
and man-made objects.

00:03:29.049 --> 00:03:34.620
Reconstruction from a pair of flash and no-flash
photography images is also a possibility and

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:39.530
the algorithm does not contain the light leaks
produced by a highly competitive handcrafted

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:42.129
algorithm, a joint bilateral filter.

00:03:42.129 --> 00:03:43.799
Quite remarkable indeed.

00:03:43.799 --> 00:03:48.650
The supplementary materials and the project
website contain a ton of comparisons against

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:52.810
competing techniques, so make sure to have
a look at that if you would like to know more.

